#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""`spe-eval`: the agent-friendly command line over `spe_eval.py` (experiments spec part 03,
`docs/specs/2026-11-10-experiments-design/03-evaluation-in-workspace.md`, and part 07's command
table): `new` to write a contribution's skeleton, `test` for the offline checks, `arm` to try one
arm by hand, `run` (with `--detach`) to measure every arm, `status`/`wait` to watch a run, `runs`
to list past ones, `results` to read one, and `pr-body` to write up the contribution (07,
*Read*/*Share*; 05-contribution.md, *The PR*).

Every command is non-interactive, prints no secrets, and exits 0 on success, 1 when the thing it
checked failed (a refused gate, a failed run, a timed-out wait, a failing test or checker problem),
2 on a usage or configuration error (also where a library call raises a plain `ValueError`, e.g. an
unknown arm or injection id).

External processes, all resolved on `$PATH` except `run.sh` and `check_experiment.py` (never
installed on it; called at their path under the experiment's own repository clone, like
`suite_config`'s `agent.yaml`):

    - `spe-apply` (bash, next to this script): `arm` and `run` apply each arm with
      `spe-apply all -e @<vars file>`, passing their own lock token as `SPE_EVAL_LOCK` so
      `spe-apply` does not refuse itself.
    - The agent's own health URL (`http://127.0.0.1:<SPE_AGUI_AGENT_PORT>/healthz`, read from
      `$SPE_FRONTEND_DIR/.env`, the same URL and the same "200 means ready" the `agui` role's own
      `checks.yml` probes): `arm`/`run` poll it directly after applying (bounded;
      `SPE_EVAL_HEALTH_TIMEOUT`/`SPE_EVAL_HEALTH_INTERVAL` override the default 120s/3s, for tests;
      `SPE_EVAL_HEALTH_URL` replaces the whole URL, for tests that have no real agent to probe).
    - `<repo_root>/platform/promptfoo-evals/run.sh`: one arm's controls-then-agent run (03, step 3),
      with `spe_eval.promptfoo_env`'s environment (`SPE_AGENT_URL`, the key file paths, promptfoo's
      telemetry/sharing/update disabled, `/usr/local/bin` on `PATH`).
    - `systemd-run` (`--detach`, R5): a transient `--user` unit named `spe-eval-<run id>`, running
      this same script's `run` command again (without `--detach`), the run id forced via
      `SPE_EVAL_RUN_ID` so the detached child uses the id already printed to the caller. `--detach`
      validates the same way `run` does and does not return until the child holds the lock (or a
      bounded wait for that gives up), so a `wait` right after never sees "no run in progress" for
      a run that is in fact starting.
    - `uv` (`test`, offline): `uv run --group dev pytest -q` in `<repo_root>/platform/promptfoo-evals`,
      the same command the workspace's `spe_eval` Ansible role installs `uv` to run (`uv sync
      --group dev` there, at creation); without `uv` on `$PATH`, falls back to `python3 -m pytest
      -q`, failing with a clear exit-2 message if that has no `pytest` either, rather than a bare
      traceback.
    - `<repo_root>/check_experiment.py` (`test`, offline): `test`'s own `<name>` when given (so a
      participant's own `spe-eval test <name>` checks the experiment they are writing, not the
      build's); otherwise the build's own experiment (`instances/local.yml`'s `spe_experiment`) if
      this is a `spe-experiments` build, else every experiment (not a build at all, e.g. a plain
      development checkout); `pr-body` also runs it, always `<name>`, never the whole catalogue.

SIGTERM/SIGINT during `run` (one `_StopState` per call, installed for the whole of `cmd_run`):
forwarded to whichever external process is currently running, and the current arm's `run.json`
always reaches a terminal state (`stopped`, here) before the lock is released, whether the signal
arrived during the apply, the health wait or `run.sh` itself.

`results`/`pr-body` only ever read `~/spe-eval/reports/` (never apply an arm or take the lock): no
run in progress blocks either of them, and neither can be refused by `LockHeld`.
"""
